  "activity": {
    "id": "act_vsepr_exploration",
    "name": "VSEPR Theory Exploration",
    "description": "An exploration of VSEPR theory and how it predicts the three-dimensional geometry of covalent molecules. Students manipulate electron domains around central atoms to minimize repulsion and observe the differences between Methane, Ammonia, and Water due to lone pair angular space.",
    "workplace": "wp_chem_vsepr",
    "start": "step_1",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_1",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Introduction to VSEPR Theory",
          "description": "Welcome to the General Chemistry AR VSEPR lab! Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion (VSEPR) theory states that electron domains around a central atom arrange themselves to minimize mutual repulsion. Look at the reference chart on the board, which shows the 5 basic electron-domain geometries. Click Next to begin our exploration of steric number 4."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "img_vsepr_chart",
              "poi": "center"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_intro",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_board",
              "type": "action",
              "augmentation": "step_2"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_intro"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_1"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Methane - Tetrahedral Geometry",
          "description": "Focus your device on the Methane (CH4) model. Carbon has a steric number of 4 (4 bonding pairs and 0 lone pairs). The bonding pairs spread out equally in 3D space to minimize repulsion, forming a perfect Tetrahedral molecular geometry. The idealized bond angle is exactly 109.5\u00b0. Click to proceed."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_methane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_methane",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_methane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_angle",
              "poi": "label_pos",
              "text": "109.5\u00b0"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_methane",
              "type": "action",
              "augmentation": "step_3"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_methane",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_2"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Ammonia - Lone Pair Repulsion",
          "description": "Now examine the Ammonia (NH3) model. Nitrogen also has a steric number of 4, but it consists of 3 bonding pairs and 1 lone pair. Because the lone pair occupies more angular space than bonding pairs, it exerts stronger repulsion. This pushes the N-H bonds closer together. The shape is Trigonal Pyramidal, with a compressed bond angle of 107\u00b0."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_ammonia",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_ammonia",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_ammonia",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_angle",
              "poi": "label_pos",
              "text": "107.0\u00b0"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_ammonia",
              "type": "action",
              "augmentation": "step_4"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_ammonia",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_3"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_4",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Water - Bent Geometry",
          "description": "Finally, focus on the Water (H2O) model. Oxygen has a steric number of 4 (2 bonding pairs, 2 lone pairs). Two bulky lone pairs exert even greater mutual and bonding-pair repulsion, squeezing the O-H bonds even closer. The resulting molecular geometry is Bent, with an observed bond angle of 104.5\u00b0."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_water",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_water",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_water",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_angle",
              "poi": "label_pos",
              "text": "104.5\u00b0"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_water",
              "type": "action",
              "augmentation": "step_5"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_water",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_4"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_5",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Review and Conclusion",
          "description": "As you have observed, while CH4, NH3, and H2O all share a Tetrahedral electron-domain geometry, the presence of lone pairs drastically alters their observed molecular shapes and bond angles. You have successfully mapped VSEPR predictions to experimental structures. Click to finish the lab."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_angle",
              "poi": "center",
              "text": "Lab Complete! All geometries originate from the same electron-domain structure, modified by lone pairs."
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_board",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_5"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
